course: geography
lesson: 12
title: The Middle East and Central Asia
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The «Middle East» is a name Europeans gave from where they stood: it is east of Europe but not as far as China, the «Far East». People who live there often prefer names such as South-West Asia and North Africa.
explain: These lands are mostly dry, so people built cities where water is: on rivers like the Tigris and Euphrates, and at oases along the old Silk Road. That is why Mesopotamia, the land between those two rivers, saw some of the first cities on Earth.
explain: The biggest city is not always the capital: Istanbul is Turkey's largest city, but its capital is Ankara; Dubai is famous, but the capital of the United Arab Emirates is Abu Dhabi.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 this map looks very different: in 1337 Mamluk Egypt rules the Levant, Persia is a great power, and Constantinople is still Byzantine until the Ottomans take it in 1453. Today's countries are much younger than those states.
explain: Think of a road trip along the Silk Road: from Istanbul across Iran to Samarkand in Uzbekistan, every stop is a city that grew where travellers and camels could find water.
fact: The Middle East usually means the lands from Egypt and Turkey to Iran and the Arabian Peninsula, and the name comes from a European view of the world.
fact: Turkey's capital is Ankara, chosen by the new republic in 1923 because it lies in the middle of the country, away from the old sultans' court, but its largest city is Istanbul, which spans Europe and Asia on both sides of the Bosporus.
fact: Istanbul was Constantinople, the Byzantine capital, which the Ottomans conquered in 1453.
fact: Iran, long called Persia in Europe, has had several capitals over the centuries, such as Tabriz and Isfahan, and today its capital is Tehran, a city of about 9 million people at the foot of the Alborz Mountains.
fact: Saudi Arabia, with its capital Riyadh, contains Mecca and Medina, the two holiest cities of Islam, and it is the largest country in the world with no river that flows all year.
fact: Iraq's capital is Baghdad on the Tigris, and Mesopotamia (a Greek name meaning «between the rivers»), the land between the Tigris and Euphrates, saw some of the first cities, such as Uruk, over 5,000 years ago.
fact: The Gulf states include the United Arab Emirates (capital Abu Dhabi, not Dubai), Qatar (Doha), Kuwait (Kuwait City), Bahrain (Manama) and Oman (Muscat).
fact: Israel's government sits in Jerusalem, which it calls its capital, but most countries keep their embassies in Tel Aviv because Jerusalem's status is disputed, and Palestinians seek East Jerusalem as the capital of a future state.
fact: Syria's capital is Damascus, one of the oldest cities in the world that has been lived in without a break, while Lebanon's capital is Beirut and Jordan's is Amman.
fact: Central Asia has five countries ending in «stan»: Kazakhstan (Astana), Uzbekistan (Tashkent), Turkmenistan (Ashgabat), Kyrgyzstan (Bishkek) and Tajikistan (Dushanbe), all part of the Soviet Union until 1991.
fact: The ending «stan» comes from Persian and means «land of» or «place of».
fact: Kazakhstan is the largest landlocked country in the world, and Uzbekistan is one of only two doubly landlocked countries, with Liechtenstein: all its neighbours have no sea coast either, so it must cross two countries to reach an ocean.
fact: Samarkand and Bukhara in Uzbekistan were great cities of the Silk Road, and Timur made Samarkand his capital from 1370.
exercise: Why is the region called the «Middle East»? | The name comes from a European view of the world | Its people chose the name long ago | It lies in the exact middle of the Earth, on the equator | It is halfway between Africa and America
exercise: What is the capital of Turkey? | Ankara | Istanbul | Izmir on the coast | Antalya
exercise: Why does Istanbul lie in two continents? | It spans both sides of the Bosporus, between Europe and Asia | It was moved from Europe to Asia in 1923 | It sits on the border of Africa and Asia, right beside the Suez Canal | Its suburbs reach across the Mediterranean
exercise: What was Iran often called in Europe? | Persia | Arabistan | Mesopotamia | Anatolia
exercise: Which two holy cities of Islam are in Saudi Arabia? | Mecca and Medina | Baghdad and Damascus | Jerusalem and Cairo | Riyadh and Tehran
exercise: What is unusual about Saudi Arabia's rivers? | It has no river that flows all year | It has the longest river in Asia | All its rivers flow underground into Iran | Its rivers freeze every winter
exercise: What does Mesopotamia mean, and where is it? | «Between the rivers», the land between the Tigris and the Euphrates | «Land of sand», the middle of the Arabian desert | «Between the seas», the land between the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf | «Mountain land», the Alborz Mountains of Iran
exercise: What is the capital of the United Arab Emirates? | Abu Dhabi | Dubai | Doha | Muscat
exercise: Which pair matches a Gulf state with its capital? | Oman and Muscat | Qatar and Manama | Bahrain and Doha | Kuwait and Riyadh
exercise: Why do most countries keep their embassies to Israel in Tel Aviv? | Because the status of Jerusalem is disputed | Because Tel Aviv is the official capital in Israel's law | Because Jerusalem has no airport | Because Tel Aviv is older than Jerusalem
exercise: Which capital is one of the oldest cities lived in without a break? | Damascus | Ankara | Astana | Abu Dhabi
exercise: What does the ending «stan» mean? | «Land of» or «place of» | «Desert» | «King of the steppe lands» | «River»
exercise: What is the capital of Kazakhstan? | Astana | Tashkent | Bishkek | Almaty, the biggest city
exercise: Until what year were the five Central Asian «stans» part of the Soviet Union? | 1991 | 1945 | 1917 | 2001
exercise: What does «doubly landlocked» mean for Uzbekistan? | It is surrounded only by countries that are also landlocked | It has two coasts on two different seas | It is the biggest country with no sea | It has two lakes inside its borders and no rivers going to the sea
exercise: Which city did Timur make his capital in 1370? | Samarkand | Baghdad | Istanbul | Tehran
exercise: Why did Silk Road cities like Samarkand and Bukhara grow up where they are? | They stood at oases where travellers found water | They were built on the sea coast for the trading ships | They stood on the coldest mountain tops | They were built by the Soviet Union in 1991
test: Leo says: «Dubai's the capital of the Emirates, obviously, it's the famous one.» What is right? | Abu Dhabi is the capital; Dubai is the most famous city | Dubai is the capital and Abu Dhabi is a small fishing village | The capital is Doha | The Emirates have no capital
test: «why didnt turkey keep istanbul as the capital, it's massive» Which is the best answer? | In 1923 the new republic chose Ankara, in the middle of the country and away from the old court | Istanbul was destroyed in 1923 | Istanbul belongs to Greece | Ankara is bigger than Istanbul and was already the old Ottoman capital, so nothing changed
test: A friend thinks Iran and Iraq are the same country spelled two ways. What is right? | They are two different countries: Iran's capital is Tehran and Iraq's is Baghdad | They are one country with two names | Iran is a city in Iraq | Iraq is the old name of Iran, used before the country changed its name in the 1930s
test: In Europa Universalis 5 Constantinople in 1337 is Byzantine. What happened to it later? | The Ottomans conquered it in 1453, and it is now Istanbul in Turkey | It stayed Byzantine until 1923 | It was taken apart and moved stone by stone to Ankara by the sultans | It was destroyed by an earthquake and never rebuilt
test: «how does uzbekistan get to the sea then?? it's stuck lol» What is right? | It must cross two countries to reach an ocean, so it trades by land, rail and road | It has a short coast on the Indian Ocean | It shares a beach with Kazakhstan on the Caspian, where its ships sail out to the ocean | It digs a canal to the Black Sea every year
test: What would happen if a desert city's only spring dried up and there was no other water? | People could not stay there unless they brought water from far away | Nothing, deserts don't need water | The city would become the capital | The sand would soon turn into rich soil and the people would grow food without water
test: «Middle East — middle of what?? stupid name» Which is the best answer? | It is the middle of the «East» seen from Europe, between the Near and Far East | It is the middle of the Earth, right where the equator crosses the Greenwich line | The middle of Africa | The middle of the Pacific Ocean
test: «Jerusalem is Israel's capital, full stop, no argument» What is the fairest answer? | Israel calls it its capital, but its status is disputed and most embassies are in Tel Aviv | Nobody claims Jerusalem as a capital | Jerusalem is the capital of Jordan | The United Nations made Tel Aviv the only capital in 1948, and nobody has argued about it since
test: In a strategy game Persia's capital moves from Tabriz to Isfahan. Is that possible in real history? | Yes, Persia's capital moved several times, and today Iran's capital is Tehran | No, Persia's capital was always Tehran, ever since the first Persian kings | No, Persia had no cities | Yes, but Persia's capital is now Baghdad, and Tehran is only a small town
test: «kazakhstan's the biggest country with no sea right» What is true? | Yes, it is the largest landlocked country in the world | No, Switzerland is bigger | No, it has a long ocean coast | No, Mongolia is bigger than Kazakhstan by a long, long way
exam: Identify the city which has served as the capital of the Republic of Turkey since the year 1923. | Ankara | Istanbul | Izmir | Bursa
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the status of the city of Jerusalem. | Israel designates it as its capital, whereas its status remains disputed and most states maintain embassies in Tel Aviv | Its status is universally recognised and undisputed | It is internationally recognised as the capital of Jordan | It has been designated by all states as a neutral zone without government
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the Republic of Uzbekistan in respect of access to the sea? | It is doubly landlocked, being bordered exclusively by landlocked states | It possesses an extensive coastline upon the Indian Ocean | It is landlocked, but borders a state possessing an extensive ocean coastline | It is an island state in the Caspian Sea
exam: Ascertain which of the following pairings of a Gulf state with its capital city is correct. | The United Arab Emirates and Abu Dhabi | Qatar and Dubai | Bahrain and Muscat | Oman and Doha
